      "math_academy_id": "q-38185",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})=3{b}_{2}-4{b}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=3{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we have $\\,T({b}_{1})=3{b}_{2}-4{b}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=3{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\,$, we have\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
